today  today i would like to talk about  freenomics  and language learning  freenomics is a term  coined by i think his name is chris  anderson  who explains how  in the modern world the cost of  distributing information on the internet  is so cheap  that some basic  economic  terms of reference a basic paradigm has  has changed or is changing  that the model increasingly will be that  things many things will be free  because the additional cost  of servicing a thousand people rather  than servicing 10 people is so low  that  in many cases if you can get one percent  of your users to pay for  a premium service  99 of the users will be able to use  a very useful service although a scaled  down range of services free of charge so  that the bulk of people  using a range of services  or products on the internet will in fact  be free users  those free users are in a sense  the marketing and the promotion  investment in order to attract that one  percent of users who want a premium  service  we have an online language learning  system called link  we use the inter the internet i didn't  build the internet i didn't invest in  the internet  the additional cost  for us to use the internet or for our  users to access link via the internet is  zero  because they already have  the internet in place which they are  using for a range of other purposes  paying their  utility bills or sending each other  sending their friends email so the cost  to use  what we offer the distribution cost is  zero  now let's get back to language learning  traditionally in language learning  people would either go to school which  can cost anywhere cost anywhere from  hundreds of dollars to over a thousand  dollars a month  or if they were self-learners  they would go and buy books and i guess  i have been a traditional  language learner i have lots of books at  home for a while i was learning korean  so here is a book it's a big thick book  beginning two that means there's a  beginning one and there's intermediate  and so forth um here's another book that  i bought on korean  i mean look at this whoops that's the  back look at the size of this book it's  a great thick book  it really doesn't get you very far but  it's some assistance okay i buy books on  russian here  um  i normally prefer smaller books this is  one colloquial korean that i bought i  bought a colloquial russian  i even bought  a  teach yourself russian and part of the  reason for that is that i have found  that it's often very useful to cover the  same ground  via different books i i'm not a great  believer in the grammatical explanations  i'm looking for content all the time so  i have in the past bought these books  nowadays that's no longer necessary  and  i make allusion to that in my book which  i wrote quite some years ago  the linguist a personal guide to  language learning it was by the way  translated into chinese it was published  in china  and the main point there was  to explain  that language learning  is something that is very individual  it's very personal  that a heavy emphasis on on listening  and reading to individual content can be  very effective provided you have the  right attitude  and in that book i spoke and this is  five six years ago about the internet as  a community  of a convivium  of learners who are learning from each  other and helping each other that world  is now  becoming more and more of a reality  uh today for example to study a language  i no longer go out and buy  books uh what i need is two things first  of all i need this  which is an ipod shuffle fifty dollars  you can also get a larger ipod if you  want i have it with me all the time  and  of course because i don't necessarily  understand what i hear  i go to link  where  there is a large library of audio  content  and text content or i can import content  from a variety of sources so essentially  what has happened is that  this little device  and whoops and and this  and the internet have replaced all these  books and of course it's much much  cheaper  so  we at link we want to take advantage of  this we want to offer this opportunity  for people to learn on their own  certain things have to happen for that  to be successful  first of all we have to make link  as attractive a place as possible for  people to learn so we have to continue  to invest in improving our functionality  our  ease of navigation on the site the  explanations that we provide and we are  continuing to do that our developers are  working constantly to improve our  functions and to make it easier and more  fun for people to use  the second thing is going back to the  original point  that we have to establish a range of  services  and functions  that are a  attractive to our free users so that  they will keep coming and tell their  friends  but b that we have a range of elite  services  specialized services that are  sufficiently attractive so that a small  percentage of our users will actually  pay  us for those additional services  and those people  will find those services sufficiently  attractive  and  sufficiently  attractive not only in terms of  functions but in terms of the cost in  other words inexpensive so they don't  mind the fact that they are in fact  supporting the other 99 of people who  are using the site for free  so that's the direction we have to go  now the 99 of people who are using the  site for free actually are helping us  because they are spreading the word  about link so that if we can continue to  increase the number of people who use  the site for free and that that if out  of that group we continue to get a  certain percentage  who are happy to pay for these premium  services  then that's the model that will make us  ultimately successful  the beneficiaries of course are the  language learners or those language  learners who who are self-motivated and  who can understand our model  so i will be doing some more uh podcasts  uh  on this subject talking about some of  the other aspects some of the other  pieces  of the uh link  model  and i look forward to questions and  comments that uh that you may have  thank you for listening

